## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design realistic E2E checkout scenarios under network stress, locale shifts, and stock race conditions. It validates understanding of deterministic mocks, proper data isolation, and robust failure handling.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end data seeding with deterministic mocks
- Real-time UI updates across viewports
- Locale and region switching with discount persistence
- Race conditions: stock changes during checkout
- Failure handling, rollback mechanisms, and idempotency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E skeleton
test('checkout with promo code', async ({ page }) => {
  await seedTestData({ locale: 'en-US', promo: 'SAVE20' });
  await page.goto('/cart');

  // Apply promo code
  await page.getByLabel('Promo Code').fill('SAVE20');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Apply' }).click();

  // Verify discount applied
  await expect(page.locator('.discount')).toHaveText('-$20.00');
  await expect(page.locator('.total')).toHaveText('$80.00');

  // Mobile viewport
  await page.setViewportSize({ width: 375, height: 812 });
  await expect(page.locator('.total')).toHaveText('$80.00');

  // Switch locale
  await page.getByLabel('Language').selectOption('de-DE');
  await expect(page.locator('.discount')).toHaveText('-€18,00');

  // Checkout with stock race condition
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await mockInventoryDecrement();
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Pay' }).click();
  await expect(page.locator('.error')).toContainText('insufficient stock');
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end tests for multi-tenant data pipelines must validate deterministic inputs, failure scenarios, and dashboard correctness under realistic conditions. These tests challenge system reliability by combining data isolation, scheduled execution, and eventual consistency requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation via headers or subdomains
- Deterministic test data using fixtures and mocks
- Idempotent reruns and retry mechanisms
- Failure injection and eventual consistency verification
- Tooling strategy between Playwright and Cypress

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright intercepts and fixtures for deterministic run
const csvPath = path.join(__dirname, 'fixtures', 'pipeline_input.csv');
await page.route('https://api.example.com/data', (route) => {
  route.fulfill({
    status: 200,
    contentType: 'application/json',
    body: JSON.stringify({ records: mockApiResponse })
  });
});

// Upload CSV fixture and configure pipeline
await page.setInputFiles('input[type="file"]', csvPath);
await page.click('[data-testid="create-pipeline"]');
await page.fill('[data-testid="pipeline-name"]', 'nightly-analytics');
await page.selectOption('[data-testid="schedule"]', 'nightly');
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests RBAC in a realistic multi-tenant admin UI with progressive permission changes, ensuring isolation and correct UI cues.

## Key Concepts
- RBAC policies per-tenant
- Test data seeding and isolation
- UI visibility vs. API enforcement
- Role upgrade propagation without relogin

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright example sketch
test('RBAC per-tenant role upgrade', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAsTenantUser(page, 'TenantA', 'u1', 'viewer');
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
  await expect(page.locator('#user-management')).toBeHidden();
  await upgradeRole(page, 'TenantA', 'u1', 'editor');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#user-management')).toBeVisible();
  await loginAsTenantUser(page, 'TenantB', 'u2', 'viewer');
  await expect(page.locator('#tenant-data')).toHaveText(/TenantA/).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle flaky network affecting role-change propagation? 
- What assertions would you add to guard against stale caches after role changes?
